<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#gamay">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gamay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gamay is a red wine grape of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the main grape of Beaujolais. In 1395 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> ordered it uprooted from the duchy's vineyards, calling it a 'very bad and disloyal' plant, to protect the reputation of Burgundy wines made from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinot-noir">Pinot noir</a>. It thrived further south in the Beaujolais, where its fruity, light wines became famous.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gamay.jpg" alt="Gamay" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Viking59 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gamay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Gamay, in full Gamay Noir à Jus Blanc, is a purple-coloured grape used to make red wines, grown most notably in Beaujolais and in the Loire Valley around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>. It is a very old cultivar, mentioned as long ago as the 15th century, and has often been cultivated because it produces abundantly. It is thought to have first appeared in the village of Gamay, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaune">Beaune</a>, in the 1360s, bringing relief to growers after the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a>. Unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinot-noir">Pinot noir</a>, it ripened two weeks earlier and was easier to cultivate, and it gave a stronger, fruitier wine in much larger quantity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In July 1395 the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, Philippe the Bold, outlawed the cultivation of the grape. He called it the disloyal Gaamez, full of very great and horrible harshness, in part because it occupied land that could be used for the more elegant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinot-noir">Pinot noir</a>. Sixty years later Philippe the Good issued another edict against it, stating that the Dukes of Burgundy were known as the lords of the best wines in Christendom and would maintain their reputation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Gamay is a very vigorous vine that tends not to root deeply on alkaline soils, which stresses the vines and gives grapes of high acidity. Carbonic maceration softens the acidity and gives notes of crushed strawberries and raspberries and of lilac and violets. Its wines are typically light-bodied and fruity, while those meant for modest ageing are made by whole-berry maceration, mostly in the Cru Beaujolais areas, and taste of sour cherries, black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and dried berry. Beaujolais nouveau is made from the more alkaline soils of southern Beaujolais.</p><p>In the Loire Valley it is typically blended with Cabernet Franc and Côt. It is also grown in the Niagara Peninsula in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake Geneva</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, and in Oregon's Willamette Valley, where Amity Vineyards introduced it in 1988. The name has been attached to other grapes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>: Napa Gamay is now known as Valdiguié. In 1929 Gamay was crossed with Seidentraube to produce the white grape Regner.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gamay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1395 Duke Philip the Bold ordered the <b>Gamay</b> grape uprooted from Burgundy’s vineyards, calling it a ‘very bad and disloyal’ plant, in favour of Pinot noir.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
